      {
        "id": 221973,
        "name": "TheBazaarClerk",
        "username": "TheBazaarClerk",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/thebazaarclerk/{size}/2156_2.png",
        "created_at": "2018-09-05T17:28:02.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Melkhor\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/m/ad7895/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Melkhor:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I would think by far the most important question to ask first here is <em>why</em> do you want it to be set in the real Tomb?In order to offer any advice on how to best progress with the story altered like that, I feel I'd first need to know why you feel it's necessary to do so in the first place?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Well, my players are newcomers to the setting and showed interest in it's take on Egypcian mythos. When it came to choose between issues 3-4-5, they seemed excited at the idea of exploring a Egypcian tomb full of mummies, traps and all those classic tidbits. I feel like making it all a scooby-doo-like hoax would take most of the fun while the twist doesn't seem worth it.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>So I'm trying to find a way to tie this adventure to the plot, while also letting players fight actual mummies and traps.&nbsp;</p>",
        "post_number": 3,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:54:46.474Z",
